        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "b046f690-c156-5b9c-bb0a-dbce6aa06cbe",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Cox Point, Garfield Glacier, Hull Bay, Marie Byrd Land, Coor Crags, Erickson Bluffs\n- Organizations: United States Antarctic Service\n- People: Richard E. Byrd\n- Topics: Antarctic exploration, geographic observation\n\nFacts:\n- Richard E. Byrd was born on October 25, 1888, and became an American naval officer who specialized in feats of exploration.\n- Cox Point is a rock headland at the southwest side of the terminus of Garfield Glacier, where Garfield Glacier discharges into Hull Bay on the coast of Marie Byrd Land.\n- From 1939 to 1941, aircraft of the United States Antarctic Service first observed and photographed Cox Point during the expedition led by Admiral Richard E. Byrd.\n- Coor Crags are several rock crags located 3.5 nautical miles southeast of Cox Point in the northern part of the Erickson Bluffs near the coast of Marie Byrd Land.\n- Richard E. Byrd died on March 11, 1957.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9c4a02e9-ad4a-56eb-bfdb-66392352d79c",
        "text": "Page: Cox Point. Sentence: Cox Point ( ) is a rock headland at the southwest side of the terminus of Garfield Glacier where the latter discharges into Hull Bay, on the coast of Marie Byrd Land.\nPage: Cox Point. Sentence: The point was first observed and photographed from aircraft of the United States Antarctic Service, 1939–41, led by Admiral Richard E. Byrd.\nPage: Richard E. Byrd. Sentence: Rear Admiral Richard Evelyn Byrd Jr., USN (October 25, 1888 – March 11, 1957) was an American naval officer who specialized in feats of exploration.\nPage: Coor Crags. Sentence: The Coor Crags ( ) are several rock crags standing 3.5 nmi southeast of Cox Point in the northern part of the Erickson Bluffs, near the coast of Marie Byrd Land.",
